items.prompt:
A colleague's utilities for a trade blotter, in £:

```cpp
struct Trade { std::string sym; int qty; double px; };

double total_notional(std::vector<Trade> trades) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i <= trades.size(); ++i)
        sum += trades[i].qty * trades[i].px;
    return sum;
}

Trade* best_trade(std::vector<Trade>& trades) {
    Trade best = trades[0];
    for (Trade t : trades)
        if (t.qty * t.px > best.qty * best.px) best = t;
    return &best;
}

void add_fee(Trade t, double fee) {
    t.px += fee;
}

Pricer* make_pricer() {
    return new Pricer();
}
```

(a) Name two problems in `total_notional`, one about cost and one about correctness. Say what each does in this browser and on a desk build. (b) What is wrong with what `best_trade` returns, and with its parameter? What happens for an empty blotter? (c) Why does `add_fee` have no effect? (d) What does `make_pricer` ask of every caller, and how would you remove that burden? (e) Write corrected signatures and bodies for all four.

items.model:
**(a)** Cost: `trades` is taken by value, so every call copies the whole blotter, every `std::string` included. It only reads, so it should be `const std::vector<Trade>&`. Correctness: `i <= trades.size()` runs one step too far and reads `trades[trades.size()]`, past the end. That is undefined behaviour. In this browser libc++'s hardening check stops the program with "vector[] index out of bounds". A normal desk build silently reads whatever follows the buffer and adds it to the total. Use `<`, or a range `for`.

**(b)** `best` is a local, so it dies at the closing brace, and `&best` is a dangling pointer. clang warns "address of stack memory associated with local variable 'best' returned". Reading through it is undefined: it may print the right trade in testing and garbage in production. The parameter is a plain `&` although the function only reads, so it rejects `const` blotters and temporaries: use `const std::vector<Trade>&`. The loop `for (Trade t : trades)` also copies every trade; use `const Trade&`. With an empty blotter, `trades[0]` reads past the end: undefined again. Return the best trade by value, wrapped in `std::optional<Trade>`, or return an index with `-1` for none. Returning by value is cheap: the compiler builds the result in place or moves it.

**(c)** `t` is a copy of the caller's trade. The fee is added to the copy, which dies at the `}`, so the caller sees nothing. The parameter must be `Trade& t`.

**(d)** It returns a raw owning pointer: every caller must remember `delete`, on every path, exactly once. One early return leaks a `Pricer`, and two deletes are undefined. Return `std::unique_ptr<Pricer>` from `std::make_unique<Pricer>()`, so ownership is in the type and the `Pricer` frees itself. Or return a `Pricer` by value if it is cheap to move.

**(e)**

```cpp
double total_notional(const std::vector<Trade>& trades) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (const Trade& t : trades) sum += t.qty * t.px;
    return sum;
}

std::optional<Trade> best_trade(const std::vector<Trade>& trades) {
    if (trades.empty()) return std::nullopt;
    Trade best = trades[0];
    for (const Trade& t : trades)
        if (t.qty * t.px > best.qty * best.px) best = t;
    return best;
}

void add_fee(Trade& t, double fee) {
    t.px += fee;
}

std::unique_ptr<Pricer> make_pricer() {
    return std::make_unique<Pricer>();
}
```
